"""Scene definitions for the Insecure Deserialization and Its Defences teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Insecure Deserialization and Its Defences',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains insecure deserialization and its defences, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] Deserialization rebuilds an object from bytes. [[slnc 300]] In Java's built-in format, the bytes name the classes to build, and some classes run code while they are being rebuilt. [[slnc 300]] So whoever writes the bytes chooses what the server builds, and some of what it runs. [[slnc 600]] Think of a flat-pack kit that comes with its own instructions, and you must follow each step while you unpack the box. [[slnc 300]] If a stranger packed the box, a stranger wrote the steps. [[slnc 600]] Our example is an online store that keeps the shopping cart in a cookie."),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['An online store keeps the cart in a cookie,', 'as a serialized Java object.', '', 'TempCleaner stands for a risky library class:', 'it deletes a marker file while it is read.', '', 'Then: ObjectInputFilter, plain data, an HMAC.'],
         narration="Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online store keeps each customer's cart in a cookie, written as a serialized Java object. [[slnc 500]] The demo uses Java's real serialization. [[slnc 300]] A class called TempCleaner stands for a risky class from a library: while it is being read, it deletes a temporary marker file, and that is as far as the demo goes. [[slnc 600]] The defences use the Java development kit's own object input filter, and its H M A C signatures."),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Serialize: an object becomes bytes', 'Deserialize: bytes become an object again', 'readObject(): the call that rebuilds it', 'Gadget: a class whose readObject runs code', 'Allow-list: the only classes that may be built', 'Signature: proof the server wrote the bytes'],
         narration='Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] To serialize is to turn an object into bytes, to store or send it. [[slnc 500]] To deserialize is to turn those bytes back into an object. [[slnc 500]] In Java, the call that does it is read object. [[slnc 500]] A gadget is a class that runs code of its own while it is being rebuilt. [[slnc 500]] An allow-list names the only classes that may be built. [[slnc 500]] And a signature proves that the server, and nobody else, wrote the bytes.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['JDK: Java Development Kit: the compiler and standard library', 'HMAC: Hash-based Message Authentication Code: a keyed checksum', 'SHA-256: Secure Hash Algorithm, 256 bits: the hash inside this HMAC', 'Base64: Bytes written with 64 printable characters, safe in a cookie', 'hex: Hexadecimal: each byte as two digits from 0-9 and A-F', 'JSON: JavaScript Object Notation: a plain text data format'],
         narration='A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] J D K is the Java development kit: the compiler and the standard library. [[slnc 400]] H M A C is a hash-based message authentication code: a checksum that only the holder of the key can make. [[slnc 400]] S H A two fifty-six is the secure hash algorithm with a two hundred and fifty-six bit result. [[slnc 400]] Base sixty-four writes any bytes with sixty-four printable characters, so they fit in a cookie. [[slnc 400]] Hex, short for hexadecimal, writes each byte as two digits, made of zero to nine and A to F. [[slnc 400]] Jason, J S O N, is JavaScript object notation: a plain text format for data.'),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['CWE: Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-502 is unsafe deserialization'],
         narration='C W E is the common weakness enumeration. Number five oh two is deserialization of untrusted data.'),
    dict(key='06-bytes-choose', kind='figure', title='The bytes choose the classes',
         body='bytes_choose_classes',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one picture. [[slnc 400]] On the left, a cookie. [[slnc 300]] It is Base sixty-four text, and under it are Java's serialized bytes. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, the classes those bytes name. [[slnc 300]] An honest cart names four: the cart, an array list, an object array, and the cart's line. [[slnc 300]] The server builds each one, and gets the cart back. [[slnc 700]] But the customer holds the cookie, and can write other bytes. [[slnc 300]] A forged cookie can name a TempCleaner instead. [[slnc 300]] The server builds that too, and the TempCleaner's own read object method runs, and deletes a file. [[slnc 700]] That is insecure deserialization. [[slnc 300]] The bytes choose what the server builds, and the server builds it before it can look."),
    dict(key='07-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='A cart becomes a cookie, and comes back',
         body='cart_cookie',
         narration='First demo: a cart in a cookie. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the cart. [[slnc 300]] Two kettles, at two thousand five hundred cents each. [[slnc 300]] And one pan, at four thousand two hundred. [[slnc 300]] The price of each line is part of the object. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the cookie. [[slnc 300]] Java writes the whole object as bytes, and the bytes are encoded as Base sixty-four text. [[slnc 300]] Three hundred and ninety-eight characters. [[slnc 700]] Look at the first six bytes, in hexadecimal. [[slnc 300]] A C, E D is the magic number of Java serialization. [[slnc 300]] Zero zero, zero five is the version. [[slnc 300]] Seven three says: a new object. [[slnc 300]] Seven two says: here is its class. [[slnc 300]] In Base sixty-four, that start is always r, capital O, zero. [[slnc 700]] When the cookie comes back, the server reads it, and gets the same cart: two lines, nine thousand two hundred cents.'),
    dict(key='08-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — A cart in a cookie',
         body='ONE. A cart in a cookie.\n  THE CART (code, quantity, price per unit in cents):\n    KTL-001  x2  2500\n    PAN-002  x1  4200\n  THE COOKIE (the cart serialized by Java, then Base64):\n    rO0ABXNyACJjb20uamsuZXhw...  (398 characters)\n  HOW IT STARTS (the first six bytes, in hex):\n    AC ED 00 05 73 72  -> Base64 rO0ABXNy\n    AC ED = the serialization magic, 00 05 = version 5\n    73 = a new object, 72 = its class description',
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The input: the cart, with a price on each line. [[slnc 400]] Then the cookie, and its first six bytes, worked out one by one.'),
    dict(key='09-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — A cart in a cookie',
         body='ONE. (continued)\n  THE CLASSES THE COOKIE NAMES (each one is built):\n    Cart, ArrayList, Object[], Cart$Line\n  THE RESULT:\n    2 lines, total 9200 cents\n    2 x 2500 + 1 x 4200 = 9200',
         narration="Reading it back, the stream names four classes. [[slnc 300]] Cart, array list, an object array, and the cart's line. [[slnc 300]] The server builds each one. [[slnc 400]] The result: two times two thousand five hundred, plus four thousand two hundred, is nine thousand two hundred cents."),
    dict(key='10-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='A lie, and a class that runs code while it is read',
         body='gadget_runs',
         narration='Second demo: the cookie decides what the server builds. [[slnc 400]] On the left, a lie. [[slnc 300]] The customer writes their own cart: two kettles, at one cent each. [[slnc 300]] The server reads it, and believes it. [[slnc 300]] The total is two cents. [[slnc 700]] On the right, something worse. [[slnc 300]] The cookie holds a TempCleaner. [[slnc 300]] It stands for a class from a library that is already on the server. [[slnc 600]] Follow the steps from the top. [[slnc 300]] Before reading, the marker file exists. [[slnc 300]] Step one: read object builds the TempCleaner, because the bytes name it. [[slnc 300]] Step two: while it is being built, its own read object method runs, and deletes the file. [[slnc 300]] Step three: only now does the server check the type, and refuse the cookie. [[slnc 600]] But the file is already gone. [[slnc 300]] Checking after the read is too late.'),
    dict(key='11-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — The cookie decides what the server builds',
         body='TWO. The cookie decides what the server builds.\n  THE FORGED CART (written by the customer):\n    KTL-001  x2  1\n  THE RESULT:\n    1 line, total 2 cents  <- A FORGED PRICE\n    2 x 1 = 2',
         narration="Here is the output, one attack per page. [[slnc 400]] The input: the customer's own cart, at one cent. [[slnc 400]] The result: one line, two times one, two cents. [[slnc 300]] A forged price is believed."),
    dict(key='12-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — The cookie decides what the server builds',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  THE FORGED COOKIE (not a cart at all):\n    a TempCleaner, path = <a marker file on the server>\n  THE MARKER FILE BEFORE READING:\n    exists: true\n  THE CLASSES THE COOKIE NAMES (each one is built):\n    TempCleaner\n  THE RESULT:\n    refused: the cookie is not a cart\n    marker file exists afterwards: false  <- IT ALREADY RAN\n  checking the type after readObject is too late',
         narration='The second attack. [[slnc 300]] The input: a TempCleaner, pointing at a marker file. [[slnc 300]] Before reading, the file exists. [[slnc 400]] The stream names one class, and the server builds it. [[slnc 400]] The result: refused, the cookie is not a cart. [[slnc 300]] But the marker file no longer exists. [[slnc 300]] It already ran.'),
    dict(key='13-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='The filter answers before each class is built',
         body='filter_checks',
         narration="Third demo: an allow-list filter. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the list. [[slnc 300]] It names the classes a cart is made of, and a few basic types. [[slnc 300]] It limits the depth, the number of objects, the array size, and the bytes. [[slnc 300]] And the last rule, exclamation mark, star, refuses everything else. [[slnc 700]] The stream asks the filter about each class, before it builds it. [[slnc 600]] On the left, the honest cart. [[slnc 300]] Four classes, all allowed. [[slnc 300]] The cart reads as before. [[slnc 600]] In the middle, the TempCleaner. [[slnc 300]] Rejected. [[slnc 300]] It is never built, so its code never runs, and the file still exists. [[slnc 600]] On the right, the forged price. [[slnc 300]] It is made only of a cart's classes, so all four are allowed. [[slnc 300]] The two cent cart is still accepted. [[slnc 300]] A filter stops unknown classes, not lies."),
    dict(key='14-act-3-1', kind='console', title='Act Three — An allow-list filter names the classes of a cart',
         body="THREE. An allow-list filter names the classes of a cart.\n  THE ALLOW-LIST (checked before each class is built):\n    Cart Cart$Line ArrayList Object String\n    Record Number Long Integer\n    maxdepth=5 maxrefs=100\n    maxarray=100 maxbytes=2000 !*  (!* = refuse the rest)\n  THE HONEST CART -> THE FILTER'S ANSWERS:\n    Cart       -> ALLOWED\n    ArrayList  -> ALLOWED\n    Object[]   -> ALLOWED\n    Cart$Line  -> ALLOWED\n    -> 2 lines, total 9200 cents",
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The allow-list, with its limits. [[slnc 400]] Then the honest cart, and the filter's answer for each class: allowed, four times. [[slnc 300]] Two lines, nine thousand two hundred cents."),
    dict(key='15-act-3-2', kind='console', title='Act Three — An allow-list filter names the classes of a cart',
         body="THREE. (continued)\n  THE TEMPCLEANER COOKIE -> THE FILTER'S ANSWER:\n    TempCleaner -> REJECTED\n  THE RESULT:\n    refused: class refused by the filter\n    marker file exists afterwards: true\n  THE FORGED PRICE -> THE FILTER'S ANSWERS:\n    Cart, ArrayList, Object[], Cart$Line -> all ALLOWED\n    -> 1 line, total 2 cents  <- STILL ACCEPTED\n  a filter stops unknown classes, not lies",
         narration="The TempCleaner cookie. [[slnc 300]] The filter's answer: rejected. [[slnc 300]] The result: refused, and the marker file still exists. [[slnc 500]] The forged price: all its classes are allowed. [[slnc 300]] So it is still accepted. [[slnc 300]] A filter stops unknown classes, not lies."),
    dict(key='16-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='Plain text, and a signature only the server can make',
         body='signed_cookie',
         narration='Fourth demo: data only, signed. [[slnc 400]] At the top left, the text of the cookie. [[slnc 300]] Version one, then each product code with its quantity. [[slnc 300]] No classes, and no prices. [[slnc 600]] The text is encoded as Base sixty-four. [[slnc 300]] That is the body. [[slnc 600]] Below, the signature. [[slnc 300]] The server computes an H M A C of the body, with S H A two fifty-six and a key only the server knows. [[slnc 300]] The cookie is the body, a dot, and the signature. [[slnc 700]] Now the customer changes the quantity from two to nine. [[slnc 300]] The server computes the H M A C of the new body. [[slnc 300]] It starts with two, one, A. [[slnc 300]] The signature in the cookie is the old one, starting with X, J, D. [[slnc 300]] They differ, so the cookie is refused, before a single line is read. [[slnc 600]] And because the cookie never carries a price, there is no price to forge.'),
    dict(key='17-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — Data only: product codes and quantities, signed',
         body="FOUR. Data only: product codes and quantities, signed.\n  THE COOKIE TEXT (no classes, no prices):\n    v1;KTL-001x2;PAN-002x1\n  THE BODY (the text in Base64):\n    djE7S1RMLTAwMXgyO1BBTi0wMDJ4MQ\n  THE SIGNATURE (HMAC-SHA-256 of the body, server's key):\n    XJD4Phz3L9opviZkyUfrNcyTrXkZnLzk2OjIBaf-zbE\n  THE COOKIE IS body.signature\n  THE RESULT (signature checked first, then prices looked up):\n    KTL-001  x2  x 2500 = 5000\n    PAN-002  x1  x 4200 = 4200\n    2 lines, total 9200 cents",
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The input: the text, then the body in Base sixty-four, then its signature. [[slnc 400]] The result: the signature is checked first, then the server looks up each price. [[slnc 300]] Two times two thousand five hundred, and one times four thousand two hundred. [[slnc 300]] Nine thousand two hundred cents.'),
    dict(key='18-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — Data only: product codes and quantities, signed',
         body="FOUR. (continued)\n  THE QUANTITY CHANGED BY THE CUSTOMER (x2 -> x9):\n    v1;KTL-001x9;PAN-002x1  (the new text)\n    the server's HMAC of the new body:\n      21AurWKCMA7UyV16wZ3AKLUrepHf-llmQ9FornieabU\n    the signature in the cookie (unchanged):\n      XJD4Phz3L9opviZkyUfrNcyTrXkZnLzk2OjIBaf-zbE\n    -> refused: bad signature\n  THE OTHER FORGED COOKIES:\n    a serialized object -> refused: not a signed cookie\n    unsigned text       -> refused: bad signature",
         narration="The changed quantity. [[slnc 300]] The server's H M A C of the new body, and the signature in the cookie. [[slnc 300]] They differ: refused, bad signature. [[slnc 500]] A serialized object is not a signed cookie. [[slnc 300]] And unsigned text fails the signature too."),
    dict(key='19-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['An allow-list needs upkeep as classes change', 'A filter stops classes, not lies', "Java's serialized form breaks easily", '  when classes change', 'Signing needs a key, and key rotation', 'A cart kept on the server avoids all of it'],
         narration="Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what the defences cost. [[slnc 500]] An allow-list of classes needs upkeep: every new field type has to be added. [[slnc 500]] A filter stops unknown classes, but not a lie told with allowed ones, like a forged price. [[slnc 500]] Java's serialized form breaks easily when a class changes, so old cookies stop working. [[slnc 500]] Signing needs a secret key, and a plan to change it. [[slnc 300]] And keeping the cart on the server, with only a session number in the cookie, avoids the whole problem."),
    dict(key='20-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['A cookie or field in Base64 starting rO0', 'readObject() on bytes from a request', 'A scanner finding: deserialization of', '  untrusted data, CWE-502'],
         narration='You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] A cookie or a form field in Base64 that starts with r, capital O, zero is a serialized Java object. [[slnc 500]] A call to read object on bytes that came from a request is this exact risk. [[slnc 500]] And a scanner finding about deserialization of untrusted data, number five oh two in the common weakness list, points at the same line of code.'),
    dict(key='21-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Never deserialize Java objects from outside', 'If you must: an allow-list filter, with limits', 'Prefer plain data with a strict format', 'Sign what the customer carries; check it first', 'The server owns prices and rights'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Do not rebuild Java objects from bytes that came from outside. [[slnc 500]] If you must, set an allow-list filter on the stream before reading, with limits on size and depth. [[slnc 500]] Prefer plain data, like JSON, with a strict format. [[slnc 500]] Sign anything that the customer carries, and check the signature before you read it. [[slnc 300]] And let the server decide prices and rights, never the cookie.'),
    dict(key='22-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['SerializedCartCookie: Java objects, with or without a filter', 'TempCleaner: the gadget: runs code while it is read', 'SignedTextCartCookie: the fix: text, an HMAC, server prices'],
         narration="Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] Everything is the Java development kit's own serialization, filter and H M A C. [[slnc 300]] Here are the classes that matter."),
    dict(key='23-class-1', kind='code', title='SerializedCartCookie — readObject, and the type check after it',
         body='// SerializedCartCookie: Java objects, with or without a filter\nif (filter != null) {\n    in.setObjectInputFilter(filter);\n}\nObject o = in.readObject();\nif (o instanceof Cart c) {\n    return c;\n}\n// too late: the object is built, its readObject has run\nthrow new CartRefused("the cookie is not a cart");',
         narration='The reader decodes the cookie and calls read object. [[slnc 300]] The type is checked only after the object is built, which is too late. [[slnc 300]] The only thing that acts earlier is the filter, set on the stream before reading.'),
    dict(key='24-class-2', kind='code', title='SerializedCartCookie — the allow-list',
         body='// SerializedCartCookie: Java objects, with or without a filter\n// ALLOW_LIST: Cart, Cart$Line, ArrayList, basic types, then\n+ "maxdepth=5;maxrefs=100;maxarray=100;maxbytes=2000;!*";\n...\nreturn ObjectInputFilter.Config.createFilter(ALLOW_LIST);',
         narration="The allow-list names a cart's classes, with limits on depth, objects, arrays and bytes. [[slnc 300]] Exclamation mark, star, at the end refuses every other class."),
    dict(key='25-class-3', kind='code', title='TempCleaner — its own readObject',
         body='// TempCleaner: the gadget: runs code while it is read\nprivate void readObject(ObjectInputStream in)\n        throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {\n    in.defaultReadObject();\n    // runs inside readObject(), before the caller sees it\n    Files.deleteIfExists(Path.of(path));\n}',
         narration='A serializable class may have its own read object method. [[slnc 300]] Java calls it while rebuilding the object. [[slnc 300]] This one deletes a file, which stands for what real gadgets from libraries do.'),
    dict(key='26-class-4', kind='code', title='SignedTextCartCookie — the signature first, then the data',
         body='// SignedTextCartCookie: the fix: text, an HMAC, server prices\nbyte[] expected = sign(body)\n        .getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII);\nbyte[] given = cookie.substring(dot + 1)\n        .getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII);\n// constant time; nothing is parsed before this passes\nif (!MessageDigest.isEqual(expected, given)) {\n    throw new CartRefused("bad signature");\n}\n...\ncart.add(m.group(1), Integer.parseInt(m.group(2)),\n        prices.cents(m.group(1)));',
         narration="The server computes the H M A C of the body, and compares it in constant time, before it parses anything. [[slnc 300]] Each line must match a strict pattern. [[slnc 300]] And the price comes from the server's own list, never from the cookie."),
    dict(key='27-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add a version to the signed cookie', 'and change the key without logging everyone out.'],
         narration="That's insecure deserialization. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] Rebuilding objects from bytes that a customer controls runs the customer's choice of code, so allow only the classes you expect before they are built, and prefer plain, signed data, with prices that come from the server. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add a version number to the signed cookie, and change the key without logging everyone out. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

